[Event "Punishing the Sicilian Pin Variation"]
[ChapterName "The Pin Variation and the move that punishes it"]
[Orientation "white"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/4pn2/8/3NP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 2 5"]

{ [%ts 0:09] We reach this after 1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 e6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 Nf6 5.Nc3. Black's ...Nf6 hits e4, and Nc3 is the main way to defend it. Now watch Black's next move: it's the Pin Variation, and you're about to learn the one move that punishes it. }
5... Bb4 { [%ts 4:00] [%cal Rb4c3,Rf6e4] The pin. Just like Bb5 in the Spanish, ...Bb4 pins the c3-knight so it no longer defends e4. Ignore it and Black simply takes the pawn. Several moves hold e4, but only one gives White a real edge. Find it. }
6. e5 $1 { [%ts 7:43] The refutation. It's forcing: the f6-knight is attacked and has to find a square. }
( 6. Nb5 { [%ts 4:43] Playable, but it's a cheap trick to answer a cheap trick: Black skips the pawn, plays ...d5 and is fine. Stjepan's move is more forcing. } )
( 6. Bd3 $6 { [%ts 6:55] It holds e4, but it's passive. Black plays ...d6 and gets a perfectly equal Scheveningen. Look for something more forcing. } )
6... Nd5 { [%ts 8:33] [%cal Gf6d5] The knight has to go to d5. The natural-looking ...Ne4 actually loses, and that's the next chapter. Now keep the initiative. }
7. Qg4 $1 { [%ts 9:26] [%cal Gg4g7] Hit g7 and ignore the pin. c3 isn't really hanging, because Qxg7 comes first. Every Black reply from here is uncomfortable. }
( 7. Bd2 { [%ts 8:46] Bd2 is playable, but Stjepan doesn't recommend it: after ...Nxc3 bxc3 Bf8 White's edge is small. Qg4 asks Black much harder questions. } )
*

[Event "Punishing the Sicilian Pin Variation"]
[ChapterName "The ...Ne4 blunder"]
[Orientation "white"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp1p1ppp/4pn2/4P3/1b1N4/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 6"]

{ [%ts 7:54] After 6.e5 you'd think it doesn't matter whether Black's knight goes to d5 or e4. It matters a lot. Watch Black play the natural ...Ne4. }
6... Ne4 $2
7. Qg4 $1 { [%ts 8:11] [%cal Gg4g7,Gg4e4] Two attacks at once: the knight on e4 and the pawn on g7. }
7... Nxc3
8. Qxg7 $1 { [%ts 8:19] c3 is hanging twice, and it doesn't matter. g7 falls and the h8-rook is next. }
8... Ne4+ $2
9. c3 $1 { [%ts 8:28] [%cal Gc3b4,Gg7h8] The discovered check is blocked by a pawn that attacks the bishop, and the h8-rook is still hanging. Black loses material either way. }
*

[Event "Punishing the Sicilian Pin Variation"]
[ChapterName "Castling walks into Bh6"]
[Orientation "white"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp1p1ppp/4p3/3nP3/1b1N2Q1/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1B1KB1R b KQkq - 2 7"]

{ [%ts 11:17] After 6...Nd5 7.Qg4, castling is the most common reply. It's also a disaster. }
7... O-O
8. Bh6 $1 { [%ts 11:24] [%cal Gh6g7] Pile up on g7. Black has to answer with ...g6. }
8... g6
9. Nde2 $1 { [%ts 11:40] Don't take the rook. Defend c3 first. The rook on f8 isn't going anywhere, and ...g6 has given you a hook. }
( 9. Nb5 { Also strong, and the engine rates it as highly as Nde2. Stjepan's move is Nde2, which simply secures c3. } )
( 9. Qg3 { Also good, and close to Nde2 by engine. Stjepan's move secures c3 first. } )
( 9. a3 { Also good by engine. Stjepan's move secures c3 first. } )
( 9. Bxf8 $6 { [%ts 11:34] Grabbing the exchange is a mistake. Black's dark squares come alive after ...Qxf8. Defend c3 first. } )
9... Re8
10. h4 $1 { [%ts 12:01] [%cal Gh4h5] The rook stepped away, so go for the hook: h4-h5 undermines g6, and Black's king position collapses. }
( 10. O-O-O { Also very strong. Stjepan goes straight for the hook on g6. } )
( 10. Qg3 { Also good. Stjepan goes straight for the hook on g6. } )
*

[Event "Punishing the Sicilian Pin Variation"]
[ChapterName "The best defence: 7...Kf8"]
[Orientation "white"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/pp1p1ppp/4p3/3nP3/1b1N2Q1/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1B1KB1R b KQkq - 2 7"]

7... Kf8 { [%ts 10:35] The engine's choice: Black gives up castling to keep g7 covered without weakening the king. The bishop still pins the c3-knight. Deal with that. }
8. Bd2 $1 { [%ts 10:35] Unpin, so c3 is properly defended. Black's best reply is ...h5, kicking the queen. Without it Black is much worse. }
( 8. a3 { Also good by engine. Stjepan's move is the simple unpin. } )
( 8. Bd3 { Also good by engine. Stjepan's move is the simple unpin. } )
8... h5
9. Qe4 { [%ts 10:56] The queen steps to a safe central square. Even against Black's best defence, White keeps a steady edge. Stjepan's summary: against the Pin Variation, remember e5 and Qg4. }
( 9. Qf3 { Also fine. Stjepan's square is e4, right in the centre. } )
( 9. Qg3 { Also fine. Stjepan's square is e4, right in the centre. } )
( 9. Qe2 { Also fine. Stjepan's square is e4, right in the centre. } )
*
